#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105720 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105575 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df7dddc6-a4fd-5b1b-9b73-8528e5224ca9" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Reciprocal Function and Simplifying the Arbitrary Constant Using the Laws of Logarithms"}] :question/id #uuid "bf47e35e-da86-5f88-b1e7-0cc731c37b31" :question/math-academy-id "e-376" :question/problem "What is the antiderivative of $\\dfrac {3+2x} {x} ?$" :question/worked-solution "We separate the integrand into the sum of two fractions so that we can simplify each term. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3+2x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{3}{x}+\\frac{2x}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{3}{x}+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3ln⁡|x|+2x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C =3 \\ln K$ where $K$ is some other constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $3\\ln|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3+2x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3ln⁡|x|+2x+C \\\\  & =3ln⁡|x|+2x+3ln⁡K \\\\  & =3ln⁡(K|x|)+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "df7dddc6-a4fd-5b1b-9b73-8528e5224ca9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049195}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485703 :question-generator/id #uuid "f6656125-a22d-56a1-99ec-39c5f69f8545" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186105720.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106053 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df7dddc6-a4fd-5b1b-9b73-8528e5224ca9" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Reciprocal Function and Simplifying the Arbitrary Constant Using the Laws of Logarithms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c3ac21a-faf1-5c31-bd11-bd0f1ae29c28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8|x|+K)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272906} {:answer/id #uuid "4f6b6e0f-74f9-56a0-8308-d6aa4f51232e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273055} {:answer/id #uuid "69c3ed4f-390d-54e1-a838-202b4306bfb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(|x|+K)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273299} {:answer/id #uuid "be5b380d-ca11-584f-b71d-959978d30a95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274036} {:answer/id #uuid "defd0299-dbd4-58d9-bf72-ce42aef07558" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{8}{3}K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f6b6e0f-74f9-56a0-8308-d6aa4f51232e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273055} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb4ad755-d29b-529f-b9a7-201a6ce07910" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274990}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4863b144-6916-5476-8b2b-08b73058b19d" :question/math-academy-id "q-15717" :question/problem "What is the antiderivative of $\\frac{8}{3x}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $\\frac{8}{3}$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\frac{1}{x},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{8}{3x}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{8}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{8}{3x}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡K) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106365 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df7dddc6-a4fd-5b1b-9b73-8528e5224ca9" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Reciprocal Function and Simplifying the Arbitrary Constant Using the Laws of Logarithms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83c63472-a761-505e-84f7-9153561670c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (K(6 + 7 \\mid x \\mid))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104339} {:answer/id #uuid "d3622d1b-baef-57de-b3ad-f20fdaee0d95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\ln (K \\mid x \\mid + 7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104340} {:answer/id #uuid "d655eaf8-cf8a-5690-8a86-e690c9ec3e2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\ln (K \\mid x \\mid) - 7x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104341} {:answer/id #uuid "43f2be20-65ec-5098-b876-aec2a5f992af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (7K \\mid x \\mid + 6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104342} {:answer/id #uuid "6e860cae-7bf5-5347-bdde-248d49098120" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\ln (K \\mid x \\mid) + 7x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104343}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b78f248e-f514-5af8-8b05-d61ee1654634" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104338}] :question/id #uuid "d467f8ff-d209-5e19-931d-0d63b82b1623" :question/math-academy-id "q-49096" :question/problem "What is the antiderivative of $\\frac{6 + 7x}{x}$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238002 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df7dddc6-a4fd-5b1b-9b73-8528e5224ca9" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Reciprocal Function and Simplifying the Arbitrary Constant Using the Laws of Logarithms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35399b5c-61ff-5ca5-be4f-4c1abe0d88b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-\\frac{3}{2x}+3ln⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236154} {:answer/id #uuid "6a533669-c40f-5a00-940e-dadb7f469489" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{3}{2x}+ln⁡(3K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236488} {:answer/id #uuid "7fce5c0b-fbff-51c0-9373-cb763d114999" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{3}{2x}+3ln⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236610} {:answer/id #uuid "8f5b6f3b-c7d8-5d5b-a12e-71a1116a0a5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-\\frac{9}{4x}+3ln⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236725} {:answer/id #uuid "c8374c9a-abe6-515a-a258-f3f08a5ed775" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-\\frac{9}{4x}+ln⁡(3K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237086}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f5b6f3b-c7d8-5d5b-a12e-71a1116a0a5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-\\frac{9}{4x}+3ln⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236725} :answer-field/id #uuid "356e1270-3ef3-5f47-b27e-aac16e288e4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237514}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "12ca3bb3-d360-53db-af13-e44e401ba3a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-103375" :question/problem "What is the antiderivative of ${(1+\\frac{3}{2x})}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Expanding the binomial and applying the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(1+\\frac{3}{2x})}^{2}\\text{d}x & =\\int (1+\\frac{3}{x}+\\frac{9}{4{x}^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\text{d}x+3\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{9}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x+3ln⁡|x|+\\frac{9{x}^{-2+1}}{4(-2+1)}+C \\\\  & =x+3ln⁡|x|-\\frac{9}{4x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=3ln⁡K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $3ln⁡|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(1+\\frac{3}{2x})}^{2}\\text{d}x & =x-\\frac{9}{4x}+3ln⁡|x|+3ln⁡K \\\\  & =x-\\frac{9}{4x}+3(ln⁡|x|+ln⁡K) \\\\  & =x-\\frac{9}{4x}+3ln⁡(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238030 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df7dddc6-a4fd-5b1b-9b73-8528e5224ca9" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Reciprocal Function and Simplifying the Arbitrary Constant Using the Laws of Logarithms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fc5000e-2b32-58d7-ad16-c7de786b85e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{ln⁡(K|x|)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236225} {:answer/id #uuid "89e62549-2f57-5be7-80e7-7b01223e1dc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ln⁡(\\frac{K|x|}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236681} {:answer/id #uuid "9ca66147-f622-51fe-8f8d-c945ee0a90c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2ln⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236797} {:answer/id #uuid "a162783a-2e70-5f31-8790-4cae86e77c64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{ln⁡(K|x|)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236827} {:answer/id #uuid "c42b65fc-6ad6-5255-ad37-78be530512d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ln⁡(2K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237057}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ca66147-f622-51fe-8f8d-c945ee0a90c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2ln⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236797} :answer-field/id #uuid "684eb99d-9162-5ff2-afed-d23e9ffc201a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237574}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "57af19c5-8aad-56d8-8498-0ea830a26c13" :question/math-academy-id "q-103603" :question/problem "What is the antiderivative of $2{x}^{-1}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $2$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\frac{1}{x},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2{x}^{-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2ln⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=2ln⁡K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $2ln⁡|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2{x}^{-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =2ln⁡|x|+2ln⁡K \\\\  & =2(ln⁡|x|+ln⁡K) \\\\  & =2ln⁡(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238031 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df7dddc6-a4fd-5b1b-9b73-8528e5224ca9" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Reciprocal Function and Simplifying the Arbitrary Constant Using the Laws of Logarithms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13a38b29-85ad-5374-bea2-88f31b9b11e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235933} {:answer/id #uuid "1b8e5d23-edd6-5e66-a10e-e0cae75992cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235989} {:answer/id #uuid "5cf58edf-2b25-5403-987e-544048dac46f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236402} {:answer/id #uuid "b6e0bbcf-015a-5615-9e6f-2fe0003bc2eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(|x|+K)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236976} {:answer/id #uuid "d4375fca-91f8-5c71-956b-c92f62e78b35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237158}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4375fca-91f8-5c71-956b-c92f62e78b35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237158} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ed20a98-5dd7-5597-b788-a5b92556c748" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237454}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "de2d042e-94ce-510a-ada4-3da6c7a2612d" :question/math-academy-id "q-103605" :question/problem "What is the antiderivative of $\\frac{2x-1}{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "We separate the integrand into the sum of two fractions so that we can simplify each term. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x-1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2x}{x}-\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2-\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x-1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =2x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C \\\\  & =2x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =2x-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡K) \\\\  & =2x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275371 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df7dddc6-a4fd-5b1b-9b73-8528e5224ca9" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Reciprocal Function and Simplifying the Arbitrary Constant Using the Laws of Logarithms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "244e842d-6715-5aeb-b13b-adc8b6cf5d73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ln⁡(\\frac{5}{2}K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272706} {:answer/id #uuid "5194416d-ecec-5f21-915f-c325c30b65c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡(|x|+K)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273082} {:answer/id #uuid "75ed1eed-b51b-5097-9cd3-a39f055f5c90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ln⁡(\\frac{5}{2}|x|+K)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273390} {:answer/id #uuid "7e002927-2ce6-5656-8e9d-e32f1ff37552" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273462} {:answer/id #uuid "c988e25e-fd28-52c7-b118-2623bf44beb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}ln⁡(5K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274136}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e002927-2ce6-5656-8e9d-e32f1ff37552" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273462} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e9cd3aa-2595-5209-9d8a-dc52f793d875" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "afa04175-238c-5ba8-a826-771eab746826" :question/math-academy-id "q-15710" :question/problem "What is the antiderivative of $\\frac{5}{2x}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $\\frac{5}{2}$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\frac{1}{x},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5}{2x}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{5}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5}{2x}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡|x|+\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡K \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}(ln⁡|x|+ln⁡K) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297249 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df7dddc6-a4fd-5b1b-9b73-8528e5224ca9" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Reciprocal Function and Simplifying the Arbitrary Constant Using the Laws of Logarithms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b9f3ccb-57a5-54e7-9651-cd8e38e071bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}ln⁡(5K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295330} {:answer/id #uuid "137963fa-e7bf-5b99-b97d-16a6e2b83b62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295376} {:answer/id #uuid "3ba78d99-70e5-5576-9ab3-f6f5d63e5557" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295610} {:answer/id #uuid "ab6cacd1-8561-54df-98e0-e7424109595d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-ln⁡(\\frac{5}{2}K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296168} {:answer/id #uuid "e35e2f64-0c17-5dfe-a27c-81e2452e3e98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡(|x|+K)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296477}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ba78d99-70e5-5576-9ab3-f6f5d63e5557" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295610} :answer-field/id #uuid "c450e82f-bb3f-51ae-bbf9-78580de51634" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297036}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eec65b66-7ead-5d6e-b179-5aacd453b9d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-29834" :question/problem "What is the antiderivative of $-\\frac{5}{2x}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $-\\frac{5}{2}$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\frac{1}{x},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -\\frac{5}{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -\\frac{5}{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡|x|-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡K \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}(ln⁡|x|+ln⁡K) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332658 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df7dddc6-a4fd-5b1b-9b73-8528e5224ca9" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Reciprocal Function and Simplifying the Arbitrary Constant Using the Laws of Logarithms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "009d8131-2ffe-5c14-a378-d1f56781bded" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-\\frac{1}{x}+2ln⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329958} {:answer/id #uuid "3c89723c-b495-521d-8ad7-e34b357b5559" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{x}+2ln⁡(|K|x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330487} {:answer/id #uuid "7a074152-9317-5da0-b3eb-342d5c464ac3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+2ln⁡(|K|x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331032} {:answer/id #uuid "a03000cf-9b4c-5aa7-bc81-3a390be00d1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-\\frac{1}{x}+ln⁡(2K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331391} {:answer/id #uuid "e71d945d-91e1-5744-9e87-32bc4829e8a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+(1+ln⁡(K|x|))^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331980}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "009d8131-2ffe-5c14-a378-d1f56781bded" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-\\frac{1}{x}+2ln⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329958} :answer-field/id #uuid "47f28b39-da74-5b7f-b9d6-861abb2e2dcf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332321}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "edf6e555-e835-5bc6-9e0b-944e25006b84" :question/math-academy-id "q-49067" :question/problem "What is the antiderivative of ${(1+\\frac{1}{x})}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Expanding the binomial and applying the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(1+\\frac{1}{x})}^{2}\\text{d}x & =\\int (1+\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x+2ln⁡|x|+\\frac{{x}^{-2+1}}{-2+1}+C \\\\  & =x+2ln⁡|x|-\\frac{1}{x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=2ln⁡K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $2ln⁡|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(1+\\frac{1}{x})}^{2}\\text{d}x & =x-\\frac{1}{x}+2ln⁡|x|+2ln⁡K \\\\  & =x-\\frac{1}{x}+2(ln⁡|x|+ln⁡K) \\\\  & =x-\\frac{1}{x}+2ln⁡(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485654 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df7dddc6-a4fd-5b1b-9b73-8528e5224ca9" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Reciprocal Function and Simplifying the Arbitrary Constant Using the Laws of Logarithms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3547442c-97cf-54cc-ab1e-894010d4f12a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\ln(26K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485660} {:answer/id #uuid "61c9bb45-6b7d-56a7-aaf0-d500481f1ff6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\ln(26|x|+K)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485669} {:answer/id #uuid "6c62c403-bdb0-54f5-80d4-93c2f467f46d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{26}{3}\\ln(|x|+K)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485672} {:answer/id #uuid "df33101d-64e7-53dc-bce8-7e8ee26b929b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(\\frac{26}{3}K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485696} {:answer/id #uuid "f252b1a7-97f2-5e63-9dce-7e6d2e5ede24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{26}{3}\\ln(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485701}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f252b1a7-97f2-5e63-9dce-7e6d2e5ede24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{26}{3}\\ln(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485701} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cd9f9bf-a341-59a6-a59f-05cfe8e7d6ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "045c9a04-0733-55bf-8771-d8bc70944d9f" :question/problem "What is the antiderivative of $\\frac{26}{3x}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $\\frac{26}{3}$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\frac{1}{x},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{26}{3x}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{26}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}\\ln|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=\\frac{26}{3}\\ln K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $\\frac{26}{3}\\ln|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{26}{3x}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{26}{3}\\ln|x|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}\\ln|x|+\\frac{26}{3}\\ln K \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}(\\ln|x|+\\ln K) \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}\\ln(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485661 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df7dddc6-a4fd-5b1b-9b73-8528e5224ca9" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Reciprocal Function and Simplifying the Arbitrary Constant Using the Laws of Logarithms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e5a5e89-9037-5553-b5d2-6a4486bcb3c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(\\frac{19}{2}|x|+K)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485667} {:answer/id #uuid "92ef70a0-d452-5a59-ab1c-9d1ac5d53d59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{2}\\ln(|x|+K)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485677} {:answer/id #uuid "b93aa08c-69e4-52cc-9d3f-c40131037a1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(\\frac{19}{2}K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485689} {:answer/id #uuid "c7719ec9-9d74-54fc-b468-a3828bdba599" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\ln(19K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485692} {:answer/id #uuid "cd786feb-ef7f-59bb-a0f9-07dc918062e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{2}\\ln(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd786feb-ef7f-59bb-a0f9-07dc918062e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{2}\\ln(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485693} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c284cce-bd2c-5191-907a-2655b2eab8fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485681}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3804d021-7f16-5da7-b098-000b906fd834" :question/problem "What is the antiderivative of $\\frac{19}{2x}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $\\frac{19}{2}$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\frac{1}{x},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{19}{2x}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{19}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{2}\\ln|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=\\frac{19}{2}\\ln K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $\\frac{19}{2}\\ln|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{19}{2x}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{19}{2}\\ln|x|+\\frac{19}{2}\\ln K \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{2}(\\ln|x|+\\ln K) \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{2}\\ln(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485678 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df7dddc6-a4fd-5b1b-9b73-8528e5224ca9" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Reciprocal Function and Simplifying the Arbitrary Constant Using the Laws of Logarithms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86bdb749-6964-56bf-886e-2406ea9390fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{5}{2x}+\\ln(5K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485674} {:answer/id #uuid "9c50f0b4-82d0-5208-9c17-960f9214e291" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-\\frac{25}{4x}+5\\ln(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485682} {:answer/id #uuid "ab800146-0d13-5760-8d30-c384e390d612" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-\\frac{25}{4x}+\\ln(5K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485685} {:answer/id #uuid "abdfc4a5-3f23-5bfa-bbb0-0e2b9637518a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-\\frac{5}{2x}+5\\ln(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485686} {:answer/id #uuid "c0d7eafc-c8a2-51db-99ad-6b6307a44727" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{5}{2x}+5\\ln(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485691}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c50f0b4-82d0-5208-9c17-960f9214e291" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-\\frac{25}{4x}+5\\ln(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485682} :answer-field/id #uuid "11110ce0-9a9b-5d32-ac92-6783feda80fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485655}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "945acb46-c0e6-527b-9ef1-1687fa58f759" :question/problem "What is the antiderivative of ${(1+\\frac{5}{2x})}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Expanding the binomial and applying the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(1+\\frac{5}{2x})}^{2}\\text{d}x & =\\int (1+\\frac{5}{x}+\\frac{25}{4{x}^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\text{d}x+5\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{25}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x+5\\ln|x|+\\frac{25{x}^{-2+1}}{4(-2+1)}+C \\\\  & =x+5\\ln|x|-\\frac{25}{4x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=5\\ln K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $5\\ln|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(1+\\frac{5}{2x})}^{2}\\text{d}x & =x-\\frac{25}{4x}+5\\ln|x|+5\\ln K \\\\  & =x-\\frac{25}{4x}+5(\\ln|x|+\\ln K) \\\\  & =x-\\frac{25}{4x}+5\\ln(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485683 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df7dddc6-a4fd-5b1b-9b73-8528e5224ca9" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Reciprocal Function and Simplifying the Arbitrary Constant Using the Laws of Logarithms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21e5c4a7-4613-53bc-8ea6-cb64e6e1a292" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{31}{2x}+31\\ln(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485657} {:answer/id #uuid "3a2c94aa-08e7-5643-a702-d10e8b43ef6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-\\frac{961}{4x}+\\ln(31K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485662} {:answer/id #uuid "421528db-2521-5704-90c2-e7762a53ea69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-\\frac{961}{4x}+31\\ln(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485664} {:answer/id #uuid "89fa9854-3450-5047-8f17-80d1d324a209" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{31}{2x}+\\ln(31K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485675} {:answer/id #uuid "d8cb3738-dfa2-5c51-b6cb-012ad668eefb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-\\frac{31}{2x}+31\\ln(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485695}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "421528db-2521-5704-90c2-e7762a53ea69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-\\frac{961}{4x}+31\\ln(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485664} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6ae0fca-5dbf-5b74-9877-d3140374a28a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485700}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a66a11fb-1259-5ab3-8c0c-9c8425b6d0e8" :question/problem "What is the antiderivative of ${(1+\\frac{31}{2x})}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Expanding the binomial and applying the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(1+\\frac{31}{2x})}^{2}\\text{d}x & =\\int (1+\\frac{31}{x}+\\frac{961}{4{x}^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\text{d}x+31\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{961}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x+31\\ln|x|+\\frac{961{x}^{-2+1}}{4(-2+1)}+C \\\\  & =x+31\\ln|x|-\\frac{961}{4x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=31\\ln K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $31\\ln|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(1+\\frac{31}{2x})}^{2}\\text{d}x & =x-\\frac{961}{4x}+31\\ln|x|+31\\ln K \\\\  & =x-\\frac{961}{4x}+31(\\ln|x|+\\ln K) \\\\  & =x-\\frac{961}{4x}+31\\ln(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485688 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df7dddc6-a4fd-5b1b-9b73-8528e5224ca9" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Reciprocal Function and Simplifying the Arbitrary Constant Using the Laws of Logarithms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bdc35e3-629d-5843-a884-ae2758caddde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-\\frac{225}{4x}+\\ln(15K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485656} {:answer/id #uuid "3df98d15-4fa7-50ac-ac08-9b30b8a001f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-\\frac{15}{2x}+15\\ln(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485663} {:answer/id #uuid "698afe3c-54a3-5fed-97c6-8e1039b93f8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{15}{2x}+\\ln(15K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485670} {:answer/id #uuid "6a87f713-9f26-5f94-a328-4069c9403f8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{15}{2x}+15\\ln(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485671} {:answer/id #uuid "965e0b2c-0e72-5fa6-8ab7-ba7ff03d5a0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-\\frac{225}{4x}+15\\ln(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485680}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "965e0b2c-0e72-5fa6-8ab7-ba7ff03d5a0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-\\frac{225}{4x}+15\\ln(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485680} :answer-field/id #uuid "bdf9924d-2986-55ef-9f43-203ca40cf77c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485690}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b39b559e-6dd3-51ab-936e-f0e9c4871293" :question/problem "What is the antiderivative of ${(1+\\frac{15}{2x})}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Expanding the binomial and applying the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(1+\\frac{15}{2x})}^{2}\\text{d}x & =\\int (1+\\frac{15}{x}+\\frac{225}{4{x}^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\text{d}x+15\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{225}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x+15\\ln|x|+\\frac{225{x}^{-2+1}}{4(-2+1)}+C \\\\  & =x+15\\ln|x|-\\frac{225}{4x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=15\\ln K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $15\\ln|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(1+\\frac{15}{2x})}^{2}\\text{d}x & =x-\\frac{225}{4x}+15\\ln|x|+15\\ln K \\\\  & =x-\\frac{225}{4x}+15(\\ln|x|+\\ln K) \\\\  & =x-\\frac{225}{4x}+15\\ln(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485694 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df7dddc6-a4fd-5b1b-9b73-8528e5224ca9" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Reciprocal Function and Simplifying the Arbitrary Constant Using the Laws of Logarithms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cc7c798-c28f-5e72-9322-1ee1aac6135f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+\\ln(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485659} {:answer/id #uuid "4e6a9723-c590-529f-a3c3-7b89d54f579e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13x-\\ln(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485665} {:answer/id #uuid "50b043d7-e27f-504b-a749-c29451b309d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13x+\\ln(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485666} {:answer/id #uuid "71ef7a94-585b-5a3c-9918-618922f2ff8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-\\ln(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485673} {:answer/id #uuid "ab14b5fb-4ed1-5e1c-b145-ce07c95f72d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13x+\\ln(|x|+K)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e6a9723-c590-529f-a3c3-7b89d54f579e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13x-\\ln(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485665} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f4bba06-ef88-5964-ab10-7440bec05827" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485668}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cff54124-30c2-5574-bb39-923719275223" :question/problem "What is the antiderivative of $\\frac{13x-1}{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "We separate the integrand into the sum of two fractions so that we can simplify each term. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{13x-1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{13x}{x}-\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (13-\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =13\\int \\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =13x-\\ln|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=-\\ln K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $-\\ln|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{13x-1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =13x-\\ln|x|+C \\\\  & =13x-\\ln|x|-\\ln K \\\\  & =13x-(\\ln|x|+\\ln K) \\\\  & =13x-\\ln(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485699 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df7dddc6-a4fd-5b1b-9b73-8528e5224ca9" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Reciprocal Function and Simplifying the Arbitrary Constant Using the Laws of Logarithms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9601d56b-13dc-5c3b-b8fc-ac4cf5a63f1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-\\frac{13}{2x}+13\\ln(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485679} {:answer/id #uuid "b21f79af-fa86-52c4-a824-e9a0f9084cc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{13}{2x}+13\\ln(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485687} {:answer/id #uuid "e1269c07-5445-54f2-bdda-222c26fdc846" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-\\frac{169}{4x}+\\ln(13K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485697} {:answer/id #uuid "e26ad8e0-8171-5fa1-8ab4-1edce19f58de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-\\frac{169}{4x}+13\\ln(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485698} {:answer/id #uuid "fbf3f2a0-70ac-5190-980d-d5f380321b26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{13}{2x}+\\ln(13K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485702}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e26ad8e0-8171-5fa1-8ab4-1edce19f58de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-\\frac{169}{4x}+13\\ln(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485698} :answer-field/id #uuid "27a6c73a-a091-5778-bc39-461ebe695de9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485658}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e28c721a-f526-5bc6-baf1-6b2e23923597" :question/problem "What is the antiderivative of ${(1+\\frac{13}{2x})}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Expanding the binomial and applying the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(1+\\frac{13}{2x})}^{2}\\text{d}x & =\\int (1+\\frac{13}{x}+\\frac{169}{4{x}^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\text{d}x+13\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{169}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x+13\\ln|x|+\\frac{169{x}^{-2+1}}{4(-2+1)}+C \\\\  & =x+13\\ln|x|-\\frac{169}{4x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=13\\ln K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $13\\ln|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(1+\\frac{13}{2x})}^{2}\\text{d}x & =x-\\frac{169}{4x}+13\\ln|x|+13\\ln K \\\\  & =x-\\frac{169}{4x}+13(\\ln|x|+\\ln K) \\\\  & =x-\\frac{169}{4x}+13\\ln(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Reciprocal Function and Simplifying the Arbitrary Constant Using the Laws of Logarithms"}]}
